    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining. i tried doing it but it's hard to tell if my cords are vibrating or not while i'm making the sound. is there like a physical way to feel the difference? also do the sounds themselves change much besides the vibration? like is one \"noisier\" than the other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a great question! Physically, you can feel a slight exhalation from your vocal cords when they vibrate for /z/, while no such sensation occurs for /s/ sounds since the vibrations don't happen.\n\nBesides the vibration, the two sounds do have their unique acoustic properties. Generally, /z/ might sound slightly \"softer\" or \"buzzier\" due to the vibration, while /s/ might sound \"razier\" or \"hissing\" because it relies purely on the turbulent airflow. However, the perception of noisiness is quite subjective, so it can vary from person to person. Practicing in front of a mirror or recording yourself might help you notice the subtle differences in airflow and tongue position. As you work on these distinctions, it will become second nature. Keep it up, and you'll continue improving!",
