    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so the /s/ is more like a hiss and the /z/ is more of a buzz... i get that. it's just hard to imagine. can u give me some examples of common words where the difference is really obvious? like words where the change in voicing completely changes the meaning?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! Consider the following pairs of words to see how the voicing of the alveolar fricative changes meaning significantly:\n\n- \"sip\" (unvoiced /s/) vs. \"zip\" (voiced /z/): Listen carefully, as the voiced version has that buzzing quality throughout the entire vowel.\n\n- \"sin\" (unvoiced /s/) vs. \"zin\" (voiced /z/): The voicing here changes the continuity of the vowel sound.\n\n- \"sir\" (unvoiced /s/ or voiced before voiced sound) vs. \"zer\" (voiced /z/ before /ɛər/): While \"sir\" tends to have a slight hissing quality due to rapid switching, the \"zer\" in \"dreams\" is definitely buzzing.\n\nThey're subtle, but each pair contains those small but impactful details perceived mainly through hearing, rather than touch. Try pronouncing these pairs softly, then switch to your full voicing, and you'll hear the mechanical difference—the buzzing and the hissing. Pay attention to how the articulators and your vocal cords contribute to each vowel. Enjoy discovering these differences in phonetics!",
      "source": "target",
